{"archetypes":[{"name":"resume-state","focus_area":"risk-integration","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff adds persistent ship resume state and hydration paths that interact with PR, CI, OOS, fork, draft, and postmerge behavior.","prompt_body":"Investigate the new ship resume-state parsing, validation, hydration, and state-writing paths in python/run_logs.py and python/ship.py. Check whether stale, corrupt, or missing state can skip required fresh phases, resume against the wrong branch, repo, or PR, lose CI counters, or write misleading terminal state. Pay particular attention to forked, forked_target, repo_unavailable, draft, merge, OOS_PENDING, and postmerge combinations. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"git-plumbing","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The diff changes git ref, branch, and snapshot extraction behavior across Bash and Python paths.","prompt_body":"Review git command construction and ref/path validation in scripts/design-pause-load.sh, scripts/design-log-publish.sh, and the Python ship/rebase flow touched by the diff. Verify that ls-tree/show snapshot extraction handles ref names, path prefixes, NUL-delimited paths, export-ignore, empty trees, and nonzero git failures without partial installs or wrong error classification. Check that branch and base-remote logic remains compatible with forked and non-forked repositories. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"bash-strict","focus_area":"code-quality","weight":1,"rationale":"The Bash changes rely on set -euo pipefail, traps, mktemp files, process substitution, and structured failure emission.","prompt_body":"Inspect the Bash strict-mode behavior in the pause/load and publish changes. Look for cases where command substitutions, process substitutions, while-read loops, traps, clear_pause_marker, mkdir/cp, or git show redirections can bypass structured emit_load_fail handling or leave ambiguous output. Check whether multiple WARN lines, marker deletion failures, and retryable failures remain parseable by callers. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"ci-loop","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The merge loop now resumes counters and changes iteration-cap and counter persistence behavior.","prompt_body":"Trace the CI monitor, wait, rebase, fix, transient retry, merge, and already_merged loop paths in python/ship.py. Verify that iteration limits are neither off by one nor bypassed after resume, and that rebase_count, fix_attempts, transient_retries, and iteration are persisted at the right moments on OK, stalled, transient, and needs-user exits. Check interactions with PrePushConflictHandoff state preservation. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"mock-fidelity","focus_area":"code-quality","weight":1,"rationale":"The regression coverage uses extensive stubs and fake runners that could mask mismatches with real gh, git, and workflow behavior.","prompt_body":"Review the new and modified tests for whether their stubs faithfully model the real shell and Python integrations they claim to cover. Compare fake gh, fake git ls-tree/show, RecordingRunner responses, and monkeypatched PR/CI objects against production assumptions such as cwd binding, ref selection, URL validation, and nonzero command behavior. Identify gaps where tests could pass while the real workflow still fails. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
